From c3ee344638ea03597752ba35883bb8dace80093d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 13:41:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(cron): one execution books one outcome when a dead worker
 and its run both report it [daveey]

Hub incident 2026-11-12 (job kanban-hub-pin-sweep, execution 367fcebc): a drain made the
restart-safe worker exit after adopting the 12:44Z fire. The gateway waiter booked the unknown
outcome via record_unknown_worker_outcome (12:47:47), then the run's own bookkeeping booked
"cron fire ownership was lost" for the same execution (12:49:06). The single fire left
failure_streak 2 (previous run succeeded) and spent two repeat slots.

Why: mark_job_run dedupes per execution through canonical_completions, but only when the caller
passes execution_id. record_unknown_worker_outcome did not, so the later booking (which did)
could not see the earlier one. The dispatch-failure, ownership-lost-interrupted, crash-tail and
operator-cancel bookings had the same gap.

Fix: every terminal booking that knows its execution id passes it, so whichever path books first
wins and the rest are no-ops.

Regression: tests/cron/test_execution_books_once.py drives both orders (worker death then
ownership-lost, and the reverse); before the fix the first order leaves failure_streak == 2.
